LineNumber : 532
Line       : Shunem,

LineNumber : 533
Line       : Shunem, on the slope of the Hill of Moreh, became the setting for the Shunammite woman’s generous care 
             for Elisha and the Lord’s restoration of her son to life. It also stood near the Philistine camp before 
             Saul’s final battle at Gilboa. The town’s history joins scenes of national tragedy with God’s power to 
             give life, calling His people to trust Him amid both loss and uncertainty.

LineNumber : 535
Line       : 2Ki 4:8-37 One day Elisha went to Shunem, and a prominent woman…

LineNumber : 539
Line       : 1Sa 28:4-5 The Philistines gathered together and camped at Shunem…

LineNumber : 588
Line       : Jdg 5:21 The River Kishon swept them away, the ancient river,…

LineNumber : 696
Line       : The named towns, including Jezreel, Shunem, and Chesulloth, served as centers of settlement for the 
             farming communities spread throughout Issachar’s territory. Jezreel later became prominent in Israel’s 
             monarchy, particularly during the reigns of Ahab and Jezebel, showing that the land assigned in Joshua’s 
             day remained central to Israel’s history. Cities provided places of administration, protection, trade, 
             and public worship, while villages sustained the daily agricultural life of the people. God’s careful 
             distribution of the land established Israel in Canaan as a people with homes, families, boundaries, and a 
             shared calling to live faithfully before Him.

LineNumber : 813
Line       : Cabul, meaning “bound” or “displeasing,” lay in the region later associated with Solomon’s dealings with 
             Hiram of Tyre, when Solomon gave Hiram towns in Galilee as payment for materials and labor used in 
             building the temple and royal projects (1 Kings 9:10–13). The proximity of Tyre and other Phoenician 
             centers gave Asher opportunities for trade and influence, but also exposed Israel to foreign alliances 
             and idolatry.

LineNumber : 869
Line       : as far as the fortified city of Tyre,

LineNumber : 870
Line       : Tyre was a powerful Phoenician city, renowned for its defenses, trade, and seafaring influence. Its place 
             on Asher’s boundary brought Israel into close contact with a wealthy Canaanite culture. Asher later 
             failed to drive out all the inhabitants of its territory, and the tribe lived among the Canaanites of the 
             region (Judges 1:31–32).

LineNumber : 872
Line       : 2Sa 24:7 They went toward the fortress of Tyre…

LineNumber : 876
Line       : Ezk 26:4 They will destroy the walls of Tyre and demolish her towers.…

LineNumber : 942
Line       : The listed towns formed the settled communities within Asher’s tribal boundaries, providing places for 
             families, agriculture, worship, and defense. Asher’s territory reached areas associated with Sidon and 
             Tyre, though the tribe did not fully remove the Canaanite inhabitants from several coastal cities (Judges 
             1:31–32). Israel’s incomplete obedience allowed pagan populations to remain in the land, creating 
             spiritual and political pressures in later generations. God had faithfully given Asher a rich 
             inheritance, but the tribe’s lasting security depended upon living faithfully within the covenant.




